Write the code map at once for blocks the NoJIT interpreter learns (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0037)

The code map writer keeps a 4 KB buffer and writes it when it is full or
when the process ends (0002, 0016). A game on the iPad rarely ends that
way: iOS ends an app in the background with SIGKILL, and the user closes
it from the app switcher. Up to 4 KB of the newest blocks (about 500),
the ones the interpreter learned last, were lost with it, and the next
image lacked them again.

CodeMapWriter::FlushPending writes what is buffered. The interpreter calls
it after each block it learns, and NoteUnalignedAOTBlock after each block
it records for a half-barrier compile. Both happen once per block, so the
map is written a few thousand times a session at most; the JIT path keeps
the buffer. WinPad Wine's "Exportovat pro převodník" then always shares
every block played so far.

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp
index 8cff835..f4f8050 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CodeCache.cpp
@@ -175,6 +175,17 @@ void CodeMapWriter::Flush(size_t Offset) {
   Flush(Offset, Lock);
 }
 
+void CodeMapWriter::FlushPending() {
+  std::unique_lock Lock {Mutex};
+  const size_t Offset = BufferOffset.load();
+  // Past the end: a thread that filled the buffer flushes it (AppendData) and knows how much of
+  // it is valid.
+  if (CodeMapFD.value_or(-1) == -1 || Offset == 0 || Offset > Buffer.size()) {
+    return;
+  }
+  Flush(Offset, Lock);
+}
+
 void CodeMapWriter::Flush(size_t Offset, std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex>&) {
   write(*CodeMapFD, Buffer.data(), Offset);
   BufferOffset = 0;
